<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sara people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Sara are a group of related peoples of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> and the north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a>, speaking Central Sudanic languages, and form the largest ethnic group in Chad. Long raided for slaves by the northern sultanates, they were drawn into French colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> growing and army service, and became prominent in the colonial and early independent state. Chad's first president, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francois-tombalbaye">François Tombalbaye</a>, was Sara.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sara-people.jpg" alt="Sara people" width="186" height="295" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saiid at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chadgirl.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Sara, sometimes called the Kaba, also live along the southern border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chad">Chad</a> they are concentrated in the Moyen-Chari, Logone Oriental and Logone Occidental regions and parts of Tandjile, and make up roughly 30% of the population; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a>, where they live in the north-west, they are about ten percent and the fourth largest group. Their society is made up of several patrilineal clans that were once united in a single polity with a common language, identity and religion. A genetic study by Excoffier and colleagues in 1987 found them most closely related to the Kunama of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Muslim peoples of the region called the Sara Kirdi, a word for non-Muslims, and the conflict between the Kirdi and the raiders known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagirmi">Bagirmi</a> went on through the 19th century. In the early 20th century the Sara became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-equatorial-africa">French Equatorial Africa</a>, in the area the French called le Tchad utile. French schooling changed their society, as did forced labour and conscription into the French army in the World Wars. In the 1920s and 30s thousands of Sara workers were forced to travel far to the south to build the Congo-Ocean Railway, where they suffered abuse and high death rates.</p><p>At independence in 1960 the southerners were more closely tied to French institutions than the northerners, which gave them political dominance in the new state. The Sara were drawn into the civil war against populations of the north and centre. Sara figures include Noël Milarew Odingar, who overthrew Tombalbaye in the coup of 1975, and Ange-Félix Patassé, President of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a> from 1993 to 2003.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Sara live chiefly by hoe farming, with taro, yams and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweet-potato">sweet potatoes</a> as their staples, and raise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, sheep, goats, chickens and small horses. Most are Christian or follow their ethnic religion, with a Muslim minority. They speak the Sara languages, part of the Central Sudanic branch of the Nilo-Saharan family.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sara people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The south, home to the <b>Sara</b> and other peoples, was made to grow cotton for export from the 1920s, and supplied soldiers and forced labour.</blockquote><a href="/state/chad-french-colony">Chad (French colony)</a></li></ul></section>
